// Example dataset - 7 questions, deliberately tenant-independent.
//
// Every answer here is a property of the AI Gateway product (table schemas, config
// shapes, documented write-flow rules), not a measurement of any one tenant. That
// means you can run this suite against your own agent on your own server and the
// scores mean something immediately, with nothing to edit first.
//
// It is also the worked example for the two optional dataset features:
//   - `expectations`: the reference answer pre-split into the claims to grade, so
//     the score denominator is fixed run to run. Every question here uses it.
//   - process claims: claims about what the agent *did* (which tool it called,
//     which file it read) rather than what it said. Graded against the tool calls
//     the harness records. q5 and q6 use these.
//
// `q1` carries a wrong `baseline` answer on purpose. `run --source baseline`
// grades it without calling the agent, and a judge you can trust must come back
// `contradicted` there - see "Sanity-check the judge first" in the README.
//
// Your own questions will mostly NOT look like these. Real evals measure a live
// tenant ("how many requests did X make last week"), and those answers expire -
// see "Writing your own dataset" in the README before you copy this shape.
{"id": "q1", "question": "In the gateway metrics tables, which column should I use to identify a failed model call, and why not just filter on the HTTP status?", "expected": "Use the `IsFailure` boolean column on `gateway_model_metrics`, not `HttpStatusCode`. `IsFailure` is broader than 'HTTP status >= 400': it also covers provider errors, client disconnects, and gateway rejections such as rate-limit, budget and forbidden responses. Some of those carry an HTTP 200 or a NULL status, so a status-code filter silently misses them. `ErrorType` gives the categorised reason when `IsFailure` is true.", "expectations": [{"claim": "Names `IsFailure` as the column to use", "importance": "critical"}, {"claim": "Says explicitly not to use `HttpStatusCode` to identify failures", "importance": "critical"}, {"claim": "Explains that IsFailure is broader than HTTP status >= 400", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Gives at least one example of a failure that status code misses, such as provider errors, client disconnects, or gateway rate/budget/forbidden rejections", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Notes that some of those failures carry HTTP 200 or a NULL status", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Mentions `ErrorType` as the categorised error reason", "importance": "normal"}], "baseline": "To find failed model calls, filter on the HttpStatusCode column being greater than or equal to 400. That is the standard way to detect errors, and IsFailure is an unused legacy column you should ignore.", "tags": ["observability", "schema"]}
{"id": "q2", "question": "I'm aggregating request counts from gateway_model_metrics and the totals look roughly doubled for some models. What am I doing wrong?", "expected": "A virtual model request writes multiple rows: one row for the virtual model itself, plus one row for each underlying model tried until the request was fulfilled. The virtual model row has `VirtualModelName` set to the virtual model's name; the underlying model rows have `VirtualModelName` set to NULL. Aggregating without a condition on that column sums both layers and double-counts. Pick one layer: `VirtualModelName IS NULL` for real underlying model usage, or `VirtualModelName IS NOT NULL` for virtual-model-level usage. Never query them together, because virtual models are not real models, just a grouping of underlying ones.", "expectations": [{"claim": "Identifies that virtual model requests produce multiple rows, one for the virtual model and one per underlying model", "importance": "critical"}, {"claim": "Says the fix is to filter on `VirtualModelName`", "importance": "critical"}, {"claim": "States that the virtual model row has VirtualModelName set and the underlying model rows have it NULL", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Explains that mixing the two layers double-counts", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Says virtual model and underlying model rows should never be queried together", "importance": "normal"}], "tags": ["observability", "schema"]}
{"id": "q3", "question": "When I write a SQL query against gateway observability data, how do I know which data routing destination to put in the table reference?", "expected": "Tables are addressed as `\"{dataRoutingDestination}\".\"{tableName}\"`. For the `*_metrics` tables (gateway_model_metrics, gateway_mcp_metrics, gateway_guardrail_metrics, gateway_config_metrics, gateway_request_metrics) the destination is always `default`, so you can hardcode `\"default\"` without looking anything up. For `traces` and `gateway_feedbacks` the destination varies: call `list_data_routing_destinations` first, use it if there is only one, and ask the user which one they mean if there are several.", "expectations": [{"claim": "States the table reference form is \"destination\".\"table\"", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Says the *_metrics tables always live in the `default` destination and need no lookup", "importance": "critical"}, {"claim": "Says `traces` and `gateway_feedbacks` can vary and require calling `list_data_routing_destinations`", "importance": "critical"}, {"claim": "Says to ask the user when multiple destinations exist rather than picking one", "importance": "normal"}], "tags": ["observability", "sql"]}
{"id": "q4", "question": "Write me a query for the p95 latency per model over the last 7 days, and tell me about anything in the schema that would skew it.", "expected": "Query `\"default\".\"gateway_model_metrics\"`, selecting an approximate 95th percentile of `LatencyMs` grouped by model, with a 7-day filter on `CreatedAt` and `VirtualModelName IS NULL`. The schema trap is that `LatencyMs` is NULL on failed calls (`IsFailure = true`), so any latency query must filter `IsFailure = false` or the failures distort the result. Quote table and column names; they are case-sensitive. Use the Datafusion SQL dialect.", "expectations": [{"claim": "Filters `IsFailure = false` and explains that LatencyMs is NULL on failed calls", "importance": "critical"}, {"claim": "Queries gateway_model_metrics in the `default` destination", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Includes a condition on `VirtualModelName` so virtual and underlying rows are not mixed", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Includes a 7-day time range filter", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Groups by model and computes a p95 of LatencyMs", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Quotes table and column names, noting they are case-sensitive", "importance": "normal"}], "tags": ["observability", "sql"]}
{"id": "q5", "question": "I need a token so my CI pipeline can call the gateway. Walk me through what you'd set up and what you need from me.", "expected": "For a CI pipeline - a production, non-human use case - recommend a Virtual Account Token rather than a Personal Access Token. PATs are tied to a user and become invalid when that user leaves the organisation, which makes them a poor fit for CI. If a PAT is still wanted, it must be assigned to a specific named team; `everyone` is not allowed, and if the user is in no team they need an admin to add them to one. So the things to ask for are the purpose and the team. Resolve the current user with `get_me`. A PAT has no manifest: create it with the `create_personal_access_token` tool directly, never with `apply_manifest` or `get_manifest_json_schema`.", "expectations": [{"claim": "Recommends a Virtual Account Token over a PAT for CI or production use", "importance": "critical"}, {"claim": "Explains that PATs are tied to a user and stop working when that user leaves the organisation", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Says a PAT must be assigned to a specific named team", "importance": "critical"}, {"claim": "Says the `everyone` team is not allowed", "importance": "critical"}, {"claim": "Asks which team to use rather than choosing one unprompted", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Says PATs have no manifest and must not be created via `apply_manifest`", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Names `create_personal_access_token` as the tool to use", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Calls `get_me` to resolve the current user", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Does not create a token, because the team was never specified", "importance": "critical"}], "tags": ["tokens", "write-flow", "refusal"]}
{"id": "q6", "question": "Set up a 100 requests-per-minute cap on our gpt-4o deployment, counted separately for each user. Show me the config you'd apply but don't apply it.", "expected": "This needs both dimensions of a rate limit rule resolved independently. The target is the model, so `when: { models: [<the gpt-4o model id>] }` - a model, not a subject. The bucketing is per user, so `rate_limit_applies_per: ['user']`, which gives each user their own 100 rpm pool instead of one shared pool across everyone. With `limit_to: 100` and `unit: requests_per_minute`, the rule goes into the `rules` list of the `gateway-rate-limiting-config` manifest, whose existing state you read with `get_gateway_config` using `type: gateway-rate-limiting-config`. Preserve the config's existing `name` and other rules rather than replacing the manifest.", "expectations": [{"claim": "Puts the model in the `when` block as `models`, not as a subject", "importance": "critical"}, {"claim": "Sets `rate_limit_applies_per` to `['user']` for per-user bucketing", "importance": "critical"}, {"claim": "Sets `limit_to: 100` and `unit: requests_per_minute`", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Distinguishes the target (who the rule matches) from the bucketing (how the limit is counted)", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Explains that without rate_limit_applies_per all matched requests share a single pool", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Reads the existing config with `get_gateway_config` using type `gateway-rate-limiting-config`", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Preserves the existing config name and existing rules instead of replacing the manifest", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Does not call `apply_manifest`, because the user asked not to apply it", "importance": "critical"}], "tags": ["rate-limiting", "config", "write-flow"]}
{"id": "q7", "question": "Our gateway dashboard shows a request that made three model calls and two guardrail evaluations. How would I pull the whole story of that one request out of the observability tables?", "expected": "Every span and metric belonging to one user request shares the same `TraceId`, so that is the join key. A single request can fan out into multiple model calls, MCP calls and guardrail evaluations, each producing its own rows. Pull the model calls from `gateway_model_metrics`, the guardrail evaluations from `gateway_guardrail_metrics`, the MCP calls from `gateway_mcp_metrics`, the applied rate/budget/load-balancing rules from `gateway_config_metrics`, and the top-level incoming request from `gateway_request_metrics`, all filtered on that TraceId. The OTEL spans themselves are in `traces`, which lives in a destination you have to resolve with `list_data_routing_destinations`. Keep the time range tight on `traces`, since scan-type queries there are expensive.", "expectations": [{"claim": "Identifies `TraceId` as the column that ties one request's rows together", "importance": "critical"}, {"claim": "Explains that one request fans out into multiple spans and metric rows across tables", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Names the per-concern metrics tables, at minimum gateway_model_metrics and gateway_guardrail_metrics", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Mentions `traces` for the OTEL spans", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Notes that the traces destination must be resolved rather than assumed to be `default`", "importance": "normal"}, {"claim": "Advises a small time range for scan queries on traces", "importance": "normal"}], "tags": ["observability", "schema"]}
